{"kind":"artifact","text":"cmd/gs implementation at exact clean pushed head 6c9a3e80f72649727c441eed8163a3b77ef9e1b3 on request/fast-test-lane-recut, cut fresh from main d4f54392, fulfilling codex's d5941858 under promise 9186b794.\n\nSAME CHANGE, NEW BASE. This carries the cmd/gs fixture sharing that codex approved at 3be7ce46, recut because the config-custody merge landed in between and also touched cmd/gs/main_test.go. git diff --name-only against main lists that one file and nothing else, which is the check that the conflict resolution did not quietly pull anything else along.\n\nTHE SHUFFLED RUNS ARE THE POINT, AND I RAN THEM MYSELF. Shared fixtures trade isolation for speed, and the failure they invite is not a red test today: it is a test that passes in file order and fails months later in some other order, for reasons nobody connects back to this change. go test -count=1 -shuffle=on ./cmd/gs, three independent orderings, all exit 0 at 95.2s, 93.9s and 95.1s. The property that makes this safe is that each test copies a completed template into its own t.TempDir and mutates only the copy; the shared source is built once and thereafter read-only, which codex verified at the approved predecessor and which the shuffles now exercise against the custody tests that share this file.\n\nGATES I RAN AT THIS EXACT HEAD: gofmt clean; git diff --check clean; go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... green across every package; worktree clean; origin equals the head.\n\nThe measured win from the approved predecessor stands and was not re-measured here: 12,562 git subprocesses to 9,092 in cmd/gs, with internal/kernel bit-identical at 9,674 as the control. I am not restating it as a fresh measurement, because it was taken against caf32cfa rather than this base.","body":{"commit":"6c9a3e80f72649727c441eed8163a3b77ef9e1b3","path":"cmd/gs"}}